1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,472
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,472 --> 00:00:04,741
Episode 20.

3
00:00:04,741 --> 00:00:07,445
A Letter Written in Starlight.

4
00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:10,339
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,339 --> 00:00:14,048
Is a light-year a measurement of time or distance?

6
00:00:14,048 --> 00:00:15,570
Ready?

7
00:00:15,570 --> 00:00:17,676
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,676 --> 00:00:22,459
At an evening sky event, a guide points a telescope toward a star.

9
00:00:22,459 --> 00:00:26,385
Maya looks through it, then steps aside for Leo.

10
00:00:26,385 --> 00:00:29,184
Are we seeing the star right now?

11
00:00:29,184 --> 00:00:31,114
she asks.

12
00:00:31,114 --> 00:00:34,796
We are receiving its light now, the guide says.

13
00:00:34,796 --> 00:00:37,839
But the light left the star earlier.

14
00:00:37,839 --> 00:00:41,888
Light travels very fast, yet space is enormous.

15
00:00:41,888 --> 00:00:46,467
Leo thinks of a postcard that arrives weeks after a vacation.

16
00:00:46,467 --> 00:00:49,565
The picture shows an earlier moment.

17
00:00:49,565 --> 00:00:53,260
That is a helpful comparison, says the guide.

18
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:56,249
The delay comes from travel time.

19
00:00:56,249 --> 00:00:59,497
Pip asks whether stars need stamps.

20
00:00:59,497 --> 00:01:04,701
The guide tells him that light delivers the information without a postal service.

21
00:01:04,701 --> 00:01:08,804
They discuss an imaginary star ten light-years away.

22
00:01:08,804 --> 00:01:12,296
Its light would take about ten years to reach Earth.

23
00:01:12,296 --> 00:01:16,644
A light-year is a distance, not a unit for someone's age.

24
00:01:16,644 --> 00:01:18,967
Maya looks at Leo.

25
00:01:18,967 --> 00:01:22,364
So you cannot say you are twelve light-years old.

26
00:01:22,364 --> 00:01:26,779
I can if I am a very confused birthday cake, he replies.

27
00:01:26,779 --> 00:01:31,915
The guide explains that sunlight also takes time to reach us: about eight minutes.

28
00:01:31,915 --> 00:01:36,535
The club enjoys the evening sky without looking directly at the Sun.

29
00:01:36,535 --> 00:01:42,961
On the way home, Maya imagines the sky as a collection of messages with different delivery times.

30
00:01:42,961 --> 00:01:48,301
Pip writes his own message: Dear future listener, I hope you are still curious.

31
00:01:48,301 --> 00:01:52,595
Leo adds: And I hope there are snacks when the message arrives.

32
00:01:52,595 --> 00:01:55,611
Now, a little more to discover.

33
00:01:55,611 --> 00:01:58,165
Light takes time to travel.

34
00:01:58,165 --> 00:02:02,051
A light-year is the distance light travels in one year.

35
00:02:02,051 --> 00:02:07,730
When we observe distant objects, the light shows them as they were when it began its journey.

36
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:13,614
Sunlight takes roughly eight minutes to reach Earth, while light from other stars takes much longer.

37
00:02:13,614 --> 00:02:16,644
Let us hear the main idea again.

38
00:02:16,644 --> 00:02:20,747
The telescope collected light that had traveled through space.

39
00:02:20,747 --> 00:02:23,994
The club compared it to a delayed letter.

40
00:02:23,994 --> 00:02:30,312
They learned that a light-year measures distance and that looking farther into space also means seeing older light.

41
00:02:30,312 --> 00:02:33,315
Here are three useful expressions.

42
00:02:33,315 --> 00:02:36,358
You can listen, or say them with me.

43
00:02:36,358 --> 00:02:39,035
How long does the journey take?

44
00:02:39,035 --> 00:02:41,711
How long does the journey take?

45
00:02:41,711 --> 00:02:44,211
The light left earlier.

46
00:02:44,211 --> 00:02:46,711
The light left earlier.

47
00:02:46,711 --> 00:02:49,864
We are seeing a message from the past.

48
00:02:49,864 --> 00:02:53,016
We are seeing a message from the past.

49
00:02:53,016 --> 00:02:55,883
Remember our listening question?

50
00:02:55,883 --> 00:02:59,592
Is a light-year a measurement of time or distance?

51
00:02:59,592 --> 00:03:01,779
Here is the answer.

52
00:03:01,779 --> 00:03:06,399
It is a measurement of distance: how far light travels in a year.

53
00:03:06,399 --> 00:03:10,190
And here is a question just for your imagination.

54
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:14,945
If you could send your future self a short message, what would you say?

55
00:03:14,945 --> 00:03:18,111
There is more than one possible answer.

56
00:03:18,111 --> 00:03:21,263
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
